 Which one of the following is true?', '{"A": "The implementation may not work if context switching is disabled in", "B": "Instead of using fetch-and –set, a pair of normal load/store can be used", "C": "The implementation of    is wrong", "D": "The code does not implement a binary semaphore"}', 'A', 0, 1, 'gatecse-2006,operating-system,process-synchronization,normal', 0, 'filter1_vol3.pdf', 431);
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO questions (id, external_code, subject_id, topic_id, gate_year, gate_set, gate_question_number, question_type, marks, difficulty, question_html, options, correct_answer, is_mta, answer_verified, tags, has_image, source_volume, source_page) VALUES ('q_vol3_5_24_28', 'vol3-5.24.28', 'sub_operating-system', 'top_operating-system_process-synchronization', 2006, NULL, '78', 'MCQ', 2.0, 'normal', 'Barrier is a synchronization construct where a set of processes synchronizes globally i.e., each process in
 the set arrives at the barrier and waits for all others to arrive and then all processes leave the barrier. Let the
 number of processes in the set be three and  be a binary semaphore with the usual   and   functions. Consider
 the following   implementation of a barrier with line numbers shown on left.

 void barrier (void) {

         1: P(S);
